  "sourceUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2015/may/01/us-aviation-authority-boeing-787-dreamliner-bug-could-cause-loss-of-control",
  "script": "If you left a Boeing 787 turned on for 248 days straight, all four power generators would shut off at the exact same time, even mid-flight. Boeing engineers caught this in lab testing back in 2015, and aviation regulators panicked. A commercial jet could lose all electrical power simply because nobody turned it off. Wait, how does staying awake break four generators? Inside each generator control unit, a tiny software clock ticks up one hundred times every second. The programmers stored that count in a 32-bit signed integer, which caps out at roughly two billion ticks, or 248 and a half days. You see, it works like a car odometer rolling past 999,999. The moment that counter overflows, the number flips negative, and the jet's failsafe shuts off all AC power at once. Until Boeing shipped a software update, the FAA issued an official federal directive with the oldest fix in IT history: airlines were legally required to turn the entire airplane off and on again every 120 days.",
